    },
    {
      "claim": "Nicardipine, a lipophilic basic calcium-channel blocker with ester/amine functionality, is totally absorbed from a 25-cm small-intestinal segment, supporting that high absorption can occur in this structural class even when first-pass metabolism limits systemic F.",
      "card_ids": ["C133"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "Additional high-permeability records (tamoxifen >2 x10^-5 cm/s, norethisterone/ormeloxifene >2 x10^-5 cm/s, melatonin 73 x10^-6 cm/s, sakuranetin 34 x10^-6 cm/s, 7,4'-dimethoxyflavone 25 x10^-6 cm/s) and the report that nabumetone is well absorbed after oral administration reinforce that lipophilic, low-HBD molecules like the query are not absorption-limited.",
      "card_ids": ["C159","C135","C151","C168","C148","C126"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 solubility records are formulation-specific (quetiapine in Labrasol/Tween 80, tamoxifen in oil, venlafaxine HCl highly soluble) and do not provide a direct query solubility value; they neither support nor refute the query's F and do not contradict high absorption.",
      "card_ids": ["C166","C163","C141","C149","C155"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "All visible L1 human oral bioavailability records report F values at or above 25% (benazepril 28–37%, ampicillin fasted 29%, apixaban with rifampin 37–39%, norethisterone 47–73%, pivampicillin 50–89%, encainide 76–78%, tamoxifen ~100%, cefroxadine ~100%, tolmetin essentially complete); none provides affirmative evidence for F < 20%.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C16","C14","C10","C15","C19","C11","C03","C13","C22","C08","C12","C20","C02","C04","C05","C09","C17","C07","C18","C21","C06","C23"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 modified-release exposure records (quetiapine XR, tramadol SR, venlafaxine ER, melatonin CR/OROS) show measurable systemic exposure after modified-release oral dosing, and L4 provides no evidence that modified release would reduce fractional absorption below the F >= 20% threshold.",
      "card_ids": ["C98","C121","C104","C105","C77","C96","C103","C112"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C130","C127","C157","C174","C170","C144","C139","C172","C136","C158","C161","C133","C126","C159","C135","C151","C168","C148","C155"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct absolute oral bioavailability measurement for the query molecule is present in the visible records.",
    "No visible record specifically addresses the query's modified-release formulation with an F or IV-comparator measurement.",
    "L4 permeability/absorption records are for other molecules; no direct query Caco-2 or human absorption data are supplied.",
    "No direct solubility measurement for the query under modified-release conditions; L4 solubility records are formulation-specific.",
    "First-pass metabolism/clearance (including ester hydrolysis and CYP-mediated clearance) is not assessed at L4; L5 may address gut-wall efflux and intestinal metabolism.",
    "Low neutral fraction at physiological pH remains a theoretical consideration, though high-permeability basic drug analogs mitigate it."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "Predicted high (F >= 20%) because all visible L1 human F records are >=25%, L4 intestinal absorption/permeability records for structurally similar lipophilic basic amines show high absorption/permeability, and no affirmative evidence supports F < 20%; the modified-release context is not directly tested but does not undermine the assessment. Confidence remains moderate because no direct query-specific or modified-release F measurement is available and first-pass/clearance is unresolved."
}

